 ## Overview
 
-Procedural volumetric cloud rendering is a real-time technique that ray-marches a spherical cloud layer built from tileable 3D noise fields, lighting it with multiple-scattering approximations and Mie phase functions [S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering physically plausible, large-scale cloudscapes with dynamic lighting and shadows at interactive frame rates [S2, unofficial]. What sets it apart from [Mr-Voxel Volume Rendering](3874-mr-voxel-volume-rendering.md) is the use of procedural noise for density generation rather than pre-computed voxel grids, and from [Ray-marched Volumetric Fog](4248-ray-marched-volumetric-fog.md) by employing specific cloud physics (Mie scattering, multiple scattering approximations, and vertical density profiles) rather than generic volumetric fog [S1, S3, unofficial].
